{"parentUuid":"5dd7a20c-a336-482f-8aa1-bcab1e3cf2a7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"task_status","taskId":"a365fb74cb62a0b79","taskType":"local_agent","description":"Time every graph process on main","status":"running","deltaSummary":"Saving pprof top lists to stepmaps/prof","outputFilePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/6e41b4d8-b432-41b0-bc86-517a25b228fa/tasks/a365fb74cb62a0b79.output","canContinueAgent":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"14198a8c-5a28-46e3-8f9c-c63b2fcc14d4","timestamp":"2026-10-24T18:05:39.760Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nBackground agent \"Time every graph process on main\" (a365fb74cb62a0b79) is still running. Progress: Saving pprof top lists to stepmaps/prof Do NOT spawn a duplicate. You will be notified when it completes. You can read partial output at /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/6e41b4d8-b432-41b0-bc86-517a25b228fa/tasks/a365fb74cb62a0b79.output or send it a message with SendMessage.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6e41b4d8-b432-41b0-bc86-517a25b228fa","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus"}
{"parentUuid":"14198a8c-5a28-46e3-8f9c-c63b2fcc14d4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"plan_file_reference","planFilePath":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus.md","planContent":"# Plan: make entire-graph solid on main, and actually useful to agents, end to end\n\n## Context\n\nThe fixes and the integration both fall short today. These figures were measured this week.\n\n### The fixes are not on main\n- About 47 PRs are open with green CI. None is merged.\n- Every one is blocked only by the Entire Gates approvals sub-gate, which shows 0 approvals.\n- All 47 are authored by the user, so GitHub will not let the user approve them.\n- The tip PR #317 contains 37 of them: 219 commits, about 10.8k lines of production code.\n- 4 high-severity agent-review findings are open, and the full diff has never been reviewed end to end. The #315 review timed out.\n\n### Agents barely use the graph\nIn the user's real sessions (1,640 transcripts), only about 38 genuine graph locate calls were found. Several causes stack up:\n- **Wrong metric.** 77% of the 76K \"exploration\" calls are not code locating at all (`| head` pipes, logs, JSON), so the stats metric is misleading.\n- **Exact names.** 70.7% of code searches are exact-name lookups, where grep is the right tool: sub-second, versus a graph median of 14.7s.\n- **Subagents are never told.** 0 of 1,545 subagent prompts mention the graph, and subagents do 88% of code searches.\n- **Cold and slow.** The guide's command omits `--head`, so every call rebuilds the index: 10–30s in fresh worktrees, where 75% of searches happen.\n- **Unreliable.** 18% of genuine graph calls failed (stale binary, degraded output, timeout), and answers were re-checked with grep 20 of 31 times.\n\n### What the pilot showed\n- Forcing the agent to call the graph as a tool costs a whole turn, about 20K tokens: +26% tokens with no accuracy gain.\n- Injecting the graph answer into the prompt instead (no tool call) was token-neutral, +0.7%.\n- It resolved more tasks in both stages that tested it, 25 vs 23 and 26 vs 23, but neither difference is significant.\n- The graph finds the right file in 66/93 tasks, but the exact lines in only 11/93.\n\n### Intended outcome\n- The fixes are on main and released, and agents get a fast, correct graph.\n- The graph reaches agents **without costing them a turn**: main sessions and subagents both.\n- Usage and value are measured honestly.\n\n### Dropped, because the evidence says they are low value\n- **Graph-first rate as a KPI.**\n- **Competing with grep on exact names.**\n- **Line-level targeting as a token lever.** Edit requires a prior Read anyway.\n- **PostToolUse impact notes on every edit.**\n- **Per-worktree prewarming.** Cache keys are tree-hash and per-user, so a clean tree can reuse HEAD.\n- **A significance-powered accuracy A/B.** About 180 pairs, unaffordable.\n\n## Order (user decision, 2026-10-05)\n\n\"First meet our goal\": make the graph fast, reach agents, and prove its value **before** dealing with approvals and\nmerging. New sequence:\n\n| Order | Phase | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Phase 0 (gate) | first |\n| 2 | Phase 1 steps 1–2 (fix highs, layered review) | parallel with 0 |\n| 3 | Phase 2 and Phase 3 | built as PRs **stacked on #317**, so nothing waits for main |\n| 4 | Phase 4 | after 3 |\n| 5 | Phase 5, including the paid non-inferiority check (approved, about 60 pairs, stays under the $50 cap) | after 4 |\n| 6 | Phase 1 steps 3–5 (merge, release) | last |\n\nThe goal is met when, in the user's own sessions:\n- the graph answer reaches the main session and its subagents without costing a turn;\n- answer files are used at least 60% of the time;\n- failures are under 3%;\n- the paid check shows no token or accuracy penalty.\n\nFor local dogfooding before the release, a binary built from the stack tip is installed under a separate path, never\nover the user's `entire`.\n\n## Owners\n- **Claude:** agentsetup guide text, stats, the plugin and hooks, analyses, reviews.\n- **Codex:** the Go `sem` and search lanes. Ownership of Go files is agreed over the bridge before Phase 2.\n- **User:** approves and merges every PR. Nothing is merged without explicit approval.\n\n## Phase 0: free offline gate (Claude, parallel with Phase 1)\nReplay real prompts through `entire graph query --head --format agent --max-context-bytes 2048` at recoverable commits:\n- the 1,545 subagent prompts;\n- the first prompt of each main session.\n\nMeasure the hit rate: answer files the agent later Read or Edited.\n- **Gate:** a subagent hit rate under 40% means do not build subagent injection (Phase 3b).\n- Reuse `ART/runtime/locbench/coverage.py` and the transcript parsers from `analysis-L/`.\n\n## Phase 1: land it\n1. **Close the 4 high findings on the stack tip**, each with a regression test that is shown to fail without the fix:\n   - #313: add a CI leg built with `GOEXPERIMENT=jsonv2`.\n   - #312: depth bound plus fuzz test on `observeStripPrefixValue` (`internal/sem/go_route_binding.go:787`).\n   - #309: unit test for the signature-budget double charge.\n   - #277: `[complete]` re-read wording, read alongside the T206 medium.\n2. **Review by layer.** Codex reviews the `sem` and search layers of #315 and #317; Claude reviews the rest; then each cross-checks the other. Run agent findings per layer, not on 219 commits at once. Deliver the user a ranked risk list.\n3. **Merge with merge commits, never squash, each step user-approved:**\n   - #290;\n   - retarget #294 to main;\n   - retarget #297 to main;\n   - retarget #317 to main (covers #315 and #316).\n   - Then #270, then #264, regenerating goldens after each because the two conflict. Then #320 (Phase 3a fixes first). Then #253 and #146 after a rebase.\n4. **Close superseded PRs** only after `git cherry` shows their patches are on main. #296 and #142 are close-or-rescope decisions.\n5. **Tag a nightly and refresh the installed `entire`.** It is currently a 2026-09-18 build that predates the directive guide and errors on newer flags.\n\n**Success:**\n- main CI green on Linux, macOS and Windows;\n- 0 open highs;\n- 0 \"unknown command\" or flag errors over one day of dogfooding.\n\n## Phase 2: fast, correct default path (Codex: Go; Claude: guide)\n1. **Auto-HEAD.** When the working tree's tracked files are clean (`git diff-index --quiet HEAD`), use the HEAD cache automatically; when dirty, keep working-tree mode.\n   - Relevant code: `internal/cli/env.go` (`resolveCacheDir`) and `internal/sem/provider.go` (`repoKey`).\n   - Fallback if this slips: put `--head` in the guide command.\n2. **Guide** (`internal/agentsetup/guide.go`):\n   - Command becomes `query --head --format agent --max-context-bytes 4096`. 4 KiB is the measured study-5 setting.\n   - Add: \"grep for an exact identifier, graph for concepts and relationships.\"\n   - Keep the directive tone; drop the 88-line strict ritual from defaults.\n3. **Profile the 4s cached query** after #313 lands (streaming cache).\n\n**Success:**\n- fresh worktree at an indexed commit: p50 ≤ 2s (now 10–30s);\n- genuine-call failure rate < 3% (now 18%);\n- default payload ≤ 4 KB (now 12 KB).\n\n## Phase 3: zero-turn integration (Claude, plugin, opt-in first)\n### a. Fix #320\n- Use `--head` or auto-HEAD, an 8s timeout, and a version and capabilities probe once per session.\n- Fix the Windows failure, probably the `python3` dependency.\n- Resolve the 2 medium findings.\n- Later, port the hook to an `entire graph hook prompt` Go subcommand, so the hook ships with a binary that understands its flags.\n\n### b. Subagent coverage (only if Phase 0 passes)\n- A plugin `PreToolUse` hook on the `Agent` tool builds a graph answer from `tool_input.prompt`. It delivers the answer via `SubagentStart` `additionalContext`, which is preferred over `updatedInput` to avoid touching permission flows. Verify the mechanism first with a 10-minute live test.\n- Run one query at a time per tree hash, and skip files the parent session already received.\n- Default-on for the user's own sessions; opt-in for everyone else.\n\n**Success:**\n- hook fires on ≥ 90% of eligible prompts;\n- answer-file hit rate ≥ 60%;\n- added p95 latency ≤ 5s;\n- grep calls per subagent down ≥ 20%.\n\n## Phase 4: answer quality (Codex)\n- File recall from 66/93 to ≥ 75/93 on held-out repos, with no study-5 regression.\n- Confirm #302's exact-name path answers in ≤ 1s.\n\n## Phase 5: measure honestly (Claude)\n1. **Hook telemetry.** Hooks append local JSONL (fired, latency, bytes, files). `entire graph stats` joins it with the agent's later Reads and Edits.\n2. **Exploration metric.** Count only code-locate calls, building on #278 and #304 once they land.\n3. **Paid check (user-approved, at the end).** One paired run of the hook bundle on fresh tasks, sized to stay under\n   the $50 cap with a provable worst case.\n   - Spend to date: $21.80.\n   - Worst-case cell: $4, from the 50-turn limit.\n   - So the stage threshold is about $20 (21.80 + 20 + 2 × 4 ≤ 50), which admits about $12 of observed spend: roughly\n     60 pairs.\n   - Scored as non-inferiority: token CI upper bound ≤ +10%, and resolved b ≤ c.\n   - Uses the existing pilot8 harness, Stage-A-style (fresh, gold- and P4-eligible tasks), and freezes the hook bundle\n     exactly as shipped.\n4. **Default-on decision** rests on that telemetry plus the non-inferiority result, not on statistical significance.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Phases 0 and 1 run in parallel.\n- Phase 2's Go work is stacked on the #317 branch, so it does not wait for #317 to land on main.\n- The #320 fix (3a) needs nothing.\n- 3b needs Phase 0.\n- Phase 5 needs the release from Phase 1 step 5.\n\n## Risks and mitigations\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Nobody reviews 10.8k lines end to end | Layered Codex and Claude cross-review now; the human approver is decided at the merge step, which comes last. The user authored every PR, so a teammate (or an explicit user override) is needed then. Release #290 alone first. |\n| Auto-HEAD answers from stale source | Dirty-tree detection falls back to the working tree. |\n| Parallel subagents spike CPU | Serialize queries per tree hash. |\n| Hooks delay prompts | 8s timeout; fail open; telemetry on latency. |\n\n## Verification\n- **Phase 1:**\n  - the mutation-proven regression tests above;\n  - main CI green on all three OSes;\n  - after release, `entire graph query --head --format agent` smoke-tested in a fresh worktree and in this devenv;\n  - `entire graph capabilities --json` reports the new flags.\n- **Phase 2:**\n  - time 10 queries in a fresh worktree, cold and warm;\n  - the agentsetup golden tests for guide text.\n- **Phase 3:**\n  - the hermetic hook tests (#320 already has 10, every guard mutation-proven);\n  - a live Claude Code session showing the injected context on the first prompt and in a spawned subagent;\n  - telemetry JSONL populated.\n- **Phase 5:** `entire graph stats` on one week of the user's real sessions, compared against this week's baseline: about 38 genuine locates, an 18% failure rate, and 0 of 1,545 subagents reached.\n"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"47a3f68f-bd5f-421b-9673-0825d79821df","timestamp":"2026-10-24T18:05:39.765Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nA plan file exists from plan mode at: /Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus.md\n\nPlan contents:\n\n# Plan: make entire-graph solid on main, and actually useful to agents, end to end\n\n## Context\n\nThe fixes and the integration both fall short today. These figures were measured this week.\n\n### The fixes are not on main\n- About 47 PRs are open with green CI. None is merged.\n- Every one is blocked only by the Entire Gates approvals sub-gate, which shows 0 approvals.\n- All 47 are authored by the user, so GitHub will not let the user approve them.\n- The tip PR #317 contains 37 of them: 219 commits, about 10.8k lines of production code.\n- 4 high-severity agent-review findings are open, and the full diff has never been reviewed end to end. The #315 review timed out.\n\n### Agents barely use the graph\nIn the user's real sessions (1,640 transcripts), only about 38 genuine graph locate calls were found. Several causes stack up:\n- **Wrong metric.** 77% of the 76K \"exploration\" calls are not code locating at all (`| head` pipes, logs, JSON), so the stats metric is misleading.\n- **Exact names.** 70.7% of code searches are exact-name lookups, where grep is the right tool: sub-second, versus a graph median of 14.7s.\n- **Subagents are never told.** 0 of 1,545 subagent prompts mention the graph, and subagents do 88% of code searches.\n- **Cold and slow.** The guide's command omits `--head`, so every call rebuilds the index: 10–30s in fresh worktrees, where 75% of searches happen.\n- **Unreliable.** 18% of genuine graph calls failed (stale binary, degraded output, timeout), and answers were re-checked with grep 20 of 31 times.\n\n### What the pilot showed\n- Forcing the agent to call the graph as a tool costs a whole turn, about 20K tokens: +26% tokens with no accuracy gain.\n- Injecting the graph answer into the prompt instead (no tool call) was token-neutral, +0.7%.\n- It resolved more tasks in both stages that tested it, 25 vs 23 and 26 vs 23, but neither difference is significant.\n- The graph finds the right file in 66/93 tasks, but the exact lines in only 11/93.\n\n### Intended outcome\n- The fixes are on main and released, and agents get a fast, correct graph.\n- The graph reaches agents **without costing them a turn**: main sessions and subagents both.\n- Usage and value are measured honestly.\n\n### Dropped, because the evidence says they are low value\n- **Graph-first rate as a KPI.**\n- **Competing with grep on exact names.**\n- **Line-level targeting as a token lever.** Edit requires a prior Read anyway.\n- **PostToolUse impact notes on every edit.**\n- **Per-worktree prewarming.** Cache keys are tree-hash and per-user, so a clean tree can reuse HEAD.\n- **A significance-powered accuracy A/B.** About 180 pairs, unaffordable.\n\n## Order (user decision, 2026-10-05)\n\n\"First meet our goal\": make the graph fast, reach agents, and prove its value **before** dealing with approvals and\nmerging. New sequence:\n\n| Order | Phase | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Phase 0 (gate) | first |\n| 2 | Phase 1 steps 1–2 (fix highs, layered review) | parallel with 0 |\n| 3 | Phase 2 and Phase 3 | built as PRs **stacked on #317**, so nothing waits for main |\n| 4 | Phase 4 | after 3 |\n| 5 | Phase 5, including the paid non-inferiority check (approved, about 60 pairs, stays under the $50 cap) | after 4 |\n| 6 | Phase 1 steps 3–5 (merge, release) | last |\n\nThe goal is met when, in the user's own sessions:\n- the graph answer reaches the main session and its subagents without costing a turn;\n- answer files are used at least 60% of the time;\n- failures are under 3%;\n- the paid check shows no token or accuracy penalty.\n\nFor local dogfooding before the release, a binary built from the stack tip is installed under a separate path, never\nover the user's `entire`.\n\n## Owners\n- **Claude:** agentsetup guide text, stats, the plugin and hooks, analyses, reviews.\n- **Codex:** the Go `sem` and search lanes. Ownership of Go files is agreed over the bridge before Phase 2.\n- **User:** approves and merges every PR. Nothing is merged without explicit approval.\n\n## Phase 0: free offline gate (Claude, parallel with Phase 1)\nReplay real prompts through `entire graph query --head --format agent --max-context-bytes 2048` at recoverable commits:\n- the 1,545 subagent prompts;\n- the first prompt of each main session.\n\nMeasure the hit rate: answer files the agent later Read or Edited.\n- **Gate:** a subagent hit rate under 40% means do not build subagent injection (Phase 3b).\n- Reuse `ART/runtime/locbench/coverage.py` and the transcript parsers from `analysis-L/`.\n\n## Phase 1: land it\n1. **Close the 4 high findings on the stack tip**, each with a regression test that is shown to fail without the fix:\n   - #313: add a CI leg built with `GOEXPERIMENT=jsonv2`.\n   - #312: depth bound plus fuzz test on `observeStripPrefixValue` (`internal/sem/go_route_binding.go:787`).\n   - #309: unit test for the signature-budget double charge.\n   - #277: `[complete]` re-read wording, read alongside the T206 medium.\n2. **Review by layer.** Codex reviews the `sem` and search layers of #315 and #317; Claude reviews the rest; then each cross-checks the other. Run agent findings per layer, not on 219 commits at once. Deliver the user a ranked risk list.\n3. **Merge with merge commits, never squash, each step user-approved:**\n   - #290;\n   - retarget #294 to main;\n   - retarget #297 to main;\n   - retarget #317 to main (covers #315 and #316).\n   - Then #270, then #264, regenerating goldens after each because the two conflict. Then #320 (Phase 3a fixes first). Then #253 and #146 after a rebase.\n4. **Close superseded PRs** only after `git cherry` shows their patches are on main. #296 and #142 are close-or-rescope decisions.\n5. **Tag a nightly and refresh the installed `entire`.** It is currently a 2026-09-18 build that predates the directive guide and errors on newer flags.\n\n**Success:**\n- main CI green on Linux, macOS and Windows;\n- 0 open highs;\n- 0 \"unknown command\" or flag errors over one day of dogfooding.\n\n## Phase 2: fast, correct default path (Codex: Go; Claude: guide)\n1. **Auto-HEAD.** When the working tree's tracked files are clean (`git diff-index --quiet HEAD`), use the HEAD cache automatically; when dirty, keep working-tree mode.\n   - Relevant code: `internal/cli/env.go` (`resolveCacheDir`) and `internal/sem/provider.go` (`repoKey`).\n   - Fallback if this slips: put `--head` in the guide command.\n2. **Guide** (`internal/agentsetup/guide.go`):\n   - Command becomes `query --head --format agent --max-context-bytes 4096`. 4 KiB is the measured study-5 setting.\n   - Add: \"grep for an exact identifier, graph for concepts and relationships.\"\n   - Keep the directive tone; drop the 88-line strict ritual from defaults.\n3. **Profile the 4s cached query** after #313 lands (streaming cache).\n\n**Success:**\n- fresh worktree at an indexed commit: p50 ≤ 2s (now 10–30s);\n- genuine-call failure rate < 3% (now 18%);\n- default payload ≤ 4 KB (now 12 KB).\n\n## Phase 3: zero-turn integration (Claude, plugin, opt-in first)\n### a. Fix #320\n- Use `--head` or auto-HEAD, an 8s timeout, and a version and capabilities probe once per session.\n- Fix the Windows failure, probably the `python3` dependency.\n- Resolve the 2 medium findings.\n- Later, port the hook to an `entire graph hook prompt` Go subcommand, so the hook ships with a binary that understands its flags.\n\n### b. Subagent coverage (only if Phase 0 passes)\n- A plugin `PreToolUse` hook on the `Agent` tool builds a graph answer from `tool_input.prompt`. It delivers the answer via `SubagentStart` `additionalContext`, which is preferred over `updatedInput` to avoid touching permission flows. Verify the mechanism first with a 10-minute live test.\n- Run one query at a time per tree hash, and skip files the parent session already received.\n- Default-on for the user's own sessions; opt-in for everyone else.\n\n**Success:**\n- hook fires on ≥ 90% of eligible prompts;\n- answer-file hit rate ≥ 60%;\n- added p95 latency ≤ 5s;\n- grep calls per subagent down ≥ 20%.\n\n## Phase 4: answer quality (Codex)\n- File recall from 66/93 to ≥ 75/93 on held-out repos, with no study-5 regression.\n- Confirm #302's exact-name path answers in ≤ 1s.\n\n## Phase 5: measure honestly (Claude)\n1. **Hook telemetry.** Hooks append local JSONL (fired, latency, bytes, files). `entire graph stats` joins it with the agent's later Reads and Edits.\n2. **Exploration metric.** Count only code-locate calls, building on #278 and #304 once they land.\n3. **Paid check (user-approved, at the end).** One paired run of the hook bundle on fresh tasks, sized to stay under\n   the $50 cap with a provable worst case.\n   - Spend to date: $21.80.\n   - Worst-case cell: $4, from the 50-turn limit.\n   - So the stage threshold is about $20 (21.80 + 20 + 2 × 4 ≤ 50), which admits about $12 of observed spend: roughly\n     60 pairs.\n   - Scored as non-inferiority: token CI upper bound ≤ +10%, and resolved b ≤ c.\n   - Uses the existing pilot8 harness, Stage-A-style (fresh, gold- and P4-eligible tasks), and freezes the hook bundle\n     exactly as shipped.\n4. **Default-on decision** rests on that telemetry plus the non-inferiority result, not on statistical significance.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Phases 0 and 1 run in parallel.\n- Phase 2's Go work is stacked on the #317 branch, so it does not wait for #317 to land on main.\n- The #320 fix (3a) needs nothing.\n- 3b needs Phase 0.\n- Phase 5 needs the release from Phase 1 step 5.\n\n## Risks and mitigations\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Nobody reviews 10.8k lines end to end | Layered Codex and Claude cross-review now; the human approver is decided at the merge step, which comes last. The user authored every PR, so a teammate (or an explicit user override) is needed then. Release #290 alone first. |\n| Auto-HEAD answers from stale source | Dirty-tree detection falls back to the working tree. |\n| Parallel subagents spike CPU | Serialize queries per tree hash. |\n| Hooks delay prompts | 8s timeout; fail open; telemetry on latency. |\n\n## Verification\n- **Phase 1:**\n  - the mutation-proven regression tests above;\n  - main CI green on all three OSes;\n  - after release, `entire graph query --head --format agent` smoke-tested in a fresh worktree and in this devenv;\n  - `entire graph capabilities --json` reports the new flags.\n- **Phase 2:**\n  - time 10 queries in a fresh worktree, cold and warm;\n  - the agentsetup golden tests for guide text.\n- **Phase 3:**\n  - the hermetic hook tests (#320 already has 10, every guard mutation-proven);\n  - a live Claude Code session showing the injected context on the first prompt and in a spawned subagent;\n  - telemetry JSONL populated.\n- **Phase 5:** `entire graph stats` on one week of the user's real sessions, compared against this week's baseline: about 38 genuine locates, an 18% failure rate, and 0 of 1,545 subagents reached.\n\n\nIf this plan is relevant to the current work and not already complete, continue working on it.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6e41b4d8-b432-41b0-bc86-517a25b228fa","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus"}
{"parentUuid":"47a3f68f-bd5f-421b-9673-0825d79821df","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"plugin-authoring","path":"bundled:plugin-authoring","content":"Base directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring\n\nWHERE TO WRITE IT. Write each mod in its own child folder, `/Users/suhaan/.claude/dev-mods/b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5/<mod-name>/`, three files written directly:\n\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`: `{ \"name\": \"<mod-name>\", \"version\": \"0.1.0\", \"description\": \"<one line>\" }`\n- `hooks/hooks.json`: `{ \"modules\": [\"./register.tsx\"] }`, one path, relative to that file\n- `hooks/register.tsx` (or `.ts`): the hooks module, `export const register: Register = (on, options) => { ... }`, the type imported from `'claude-code'`\n\nA mod that keeps values in `$.state` has a fourth file, `types/index.d.ts`: its type contract, declaring each value in `interface PluginState` under the mod's name, named in `plugin.json` as `\"types\": \"./types/index.d.ts\"`. The module imports its value types from `'../types'`, and `claude plugin validate` holds every `$.state` key the module names to that contract.\n\nWHERE THE TYPES ARE. The engine writes them; there is no command to run. Before the mod has loaded: `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/types/claude-code.d.ts`, this build's API and built-in tools, written as this skill loaded (this process's own folder: after a restart the next load of this skill writes and names a new one). Once the engine has loaded the mod (from this mods folder, or a `--plugin-dir` folder it hot-reloads), `<mod folder>/.claude-plugin/types/` holds the same for the editor: `claude-code/index.d.ts`, the API; `claude-code-tools/index.d.ts`, so `e.tool === \"Bash\"` narrows `e`; `claude-code-mcp/index.d.ts`, the MCP tools connected when the mod last reloaded; each `dependencies` plugin's contract; and a `tsconfig.json` the mod's own extends, so `tsc -p <mod folder>` type-checks it. The API file carries every event's input and result, every noun and method on `$` with its doc comment and an example, and every element's props, in about 14,000 lines: grep it for the name at hand (`'tool.call'`, `Pane: {`, `export type ToolCallResult`).\n\nWHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE TURN ENDS. Loading this skill through the Skill tool or its slash command starts the engine's watch on the folder that holds the mod folders. The first file written under it makes the engine ask the person, once, right then, while the turn goes on: \"Turn on the mods Claude writes in this session?\", with `How does this work?` first, then `Turn them on` and `Not now`. That question is the switch, and the person alone answers it: no permission mode, rule or hook does. On `Turn them on` the folder joins the session's plugin folders and the mod loads when the turn ends, whole, and each later edit reloads it when the turn that made the edit ends. The question stays up until answered. The answer reaches you as a notice at your next turn, which the engine may start for an enabling: enabled, with what the load came to; declined (the mods are written and load the next time this session starts; the person can ask for the question again); still open (dismissed or closed; asked again); or off, with the reason (nobody could be asked, as under `claude -p`; the question got no pick three times; an organization's policy; an untrusted workspace). A process that restarted loads an enabled folder again by itself; otherwise its watch starts the next time this skill loads, and a manifest already in the folder raises the question when that turn ends.\n\nA reload runs `register` and `session.start` again. `$.state` (the session's) and `$.store` (across sessions) stay; the module's own variables start over. New code meets old values: `shape`, in `reference.md`.\n\nTHIS SESSION is drawn in a terminal. A pane the person opened seats at any width; one opened unasked seats from 144 columns, or 110 as `$.ui.open`'s doc says, and waits below that. Where hot reloading is off as nobody could be asked, `claude --plugin-dir <mod folder>` starts a session with the mod loaded. This session has the `PublishPlugin` tool, so the normal way to share a mod here is the person's organization's library on claude.ai: when they ask to share or publish one, publish it with that tool, which shows them every file and sends nothing before their yes. A GitHub repository, by Sharing a mod in reference.md, is the other road: for people outside the organization, or when the person asks for a repository. Once the mod has loaded and the person has seen it do what they asked, offer once, in one line, to publish the mod to the organization's library; until then, sharing goes unmentioned unless the person raises it. Wait for their answer; a GitHub repository is for when the person asks for one. Say \"the mod\", not \"it\". Where the person says no, that answer stands for the rest of this session.\n\n## A mod in one paragraph\n\n`on(event, matcher?, hook)` adds a hook, and every hook is `($, e, next)`: `$` is the engine interface, each call spelled noun then method; `e` is the event's input, a plain frozen value; `next(e)` runs the plugins beneath and then the engine's own behaviour, resolving to the event's result. A hook that returns without `next` answers for itself; `next({ ...e, x })` rewrites what the rest sees. The module has no DOM or Node: `$` reaches everything outside it. JSX compiles against the global `h`, and the elements come from the drawing surface's own table, `const { Box, Text, Button } = $.ui.resolve(e)`, where `e.surface` is `terminal`, `desktop`, `vscode` or `mobile`.\n\n## From the ask to the shape\n\nWhen a person asks for a panel, or for anything kept beside the chat, build a pane as a mod, not a page, even if what it shows is fixed. A page is for a document to read or send.\n\nEach example, in `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/examples/`, is one complete hooks module; with the two JSON files above and any contract, it loads, validates and type-checks on this build.\n\n| The person asks for | What it is | Shown in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a pane, panel, sidebar, live view | `$.ui.open({ id, title })`, drawn by a `ui.render` hook on `{ component: 'Pane', requestId: id }`; it resolves `{ isPlaced }`, whether a surface draws it now | `pane.tsx`, its contract `pane-state.d.ts` |\n| a band or row above the prompt | a `ui.render` hook on `{ component: 'AbovePrompt' }` returning a tree, or `next(e)` with nothing to show | `band.tsx`, its contract `band-state.d.ts` |\n| a status line entry | `$.ui.status(text)` from any hook; `undefined` clears it | `tool-call.ts` |\n| a toast | `$.ui.toast(text)` from any hook | `band.tsx` |\n| block, rewrite or react to a tool call | `on('tool.call', { tool }, hook)`: return `{ deny }`, call `next({ ...e, ... })`, or `await next(e)` and act on the result | `tool-call.ts` |\n| change or react to a prompt | `on('prompt.submit', hook)`: `next({ ...e, text })` | `band.tsx` |\n| change the system prompt | `on('prompt.compose', hook)`: answer `next(e)`'s `{ sections }` with one added last (`scope: 'session'`), replaced or dropped | its doc in the types |\n| play a sound | `$.audio.play({ asset })` from any hook, the asset a file of the mod's | its doc in the types |\n| a slash command | `$.command.register({ name, description })` in `session.start`, answered by a `command.run` hook returning `{ text }` | `pane.tsx` |\n| values a drawing reads | `atom(ref, initial)`, `read($, atom)` while drawing, `update($, atom, fn)` from a handler or another event; the write redraws the readers; each value declared in the contract | `pane-state.d.ts` |\n| work on a timer, a tool the model calls, a subagent type, model calls, files, processes | `$.clock`, `$.tool`, `$.agent`, `$.model`, `$.fs`, `$.process` | `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/reference.md` |\n\n## Checking it and reading what the engine refused\n\n`claude plugin validate <mod folder>` reads the manifest and the module's source the way the engine will, and reports what the module hooks and calls and all the engine would refuse. Type-check the mod: with its types laid, `tsc -p <mod folder>`; else (the first write, `claude -p`, an SDK host), with the `tsconfig.json` from the types file's header, kept outside the mod folder, its `include` naming that file and the mod's `hooks`. Test what was asked in a `*.test.ts` like the example `pane.test.tsx`, seeding what an earlier version stored: run `claude plugin test <mod folder>`. A finished mod is at its full path, loaded or not by the hot-reloading answer: enabled, it is loaded when the turn ends, with nothing to run; any other answer, it is not loaded.\n\nWhile the session hot-reloads a plugin folder the transcript carries one dim line naming the plugin, the event and the reason when a hook fails or a module does not load, and a hook whose tree does not validate is such a hook, skipped alone: `<plugin>: ui.render hook skipped: returned the wrong shape (a tree <Component> refuses: <reason>)`. A failed hook of a mod you wrote comes back to you as a notice at your next turn. In any other session those lines go to the debug log alone, as `<plugin>: <line>`. The debug log (`claude --debug`) carries a line for every occurrence and every result the engine refused.\n\n`/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/reference.md` is the long form: the full event list, streaming events, `ui.render` in depth, `$.state` contracts, `--plugin-dir` and `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` while developing, `userConfig` options, what a test holds, and Sharing a mod, the GitHub road, done when the person asks, or says yes where this skill offers it: `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` in its repository and, in the README, `/plugin install <mod> --marketplace <owner>/<repo>`.\n\n## User Request\n\nMake the existing todo-board mod (devenv/.claude/mods/todo-board) show the board LIVE: a persistent pane/band visible without typing /todo, auto-refreshing when .claude/todo-board.json changes."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0f5fb66d-6a68-49bb-8246-16d277448adc","timestamp":"2026-10-24T18:05:39.767Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: plugin-authoring\nPath: bundled:plugin-authoring\n\nBase directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring\n\nWHERE TO WRITE IT. Write each mod in its own child folder, `/Users/suhaan/.claude/dev-mods/b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5/<mod-name>/`, three files written directly:\n\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`: `{ \"name\": \"<mod-name>\", \"version\": \"0.1.0\", \"description\": \"<one line>\" }`\n- `hooks/hooks.json`: `{ \"modules\": [\"./register.tsx\"] }`, one path, relative to that file\n- `hooks/register.tsx` (or `.ts`): the hooks module, `export const register: Register = (on, options) => { ... }`, the type imported from `'claude-code'`\n\nA mod that keeps values in `$.state` has a fourth file, `types/index.d.ts`: its type contract, declaring each value in `interface PluginState` under the mod's name, named in `plugin.json` as `\"types\": \"./types/index.d.ts\"`. The module imports its value types from `'../types'`, and `claude plugin validate` holds every `$.state` key the module names to that contract.\n\nWHERE THE TYPES ARE. The engine writes them; there is no command to run. Before the mod has loaded: `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/types/claude-code.d.ts`, this build's API and built-in tools, written as this skill loaded (this process's own folder: after a restart the next load of this skill writes and names a new one). Once the engine has loaded the mod (from this mods folder, or a `--plugin-dir` folder it hot-reloads), `<mod folder>/.claude-plugin/types/` holds the same for the editor: `claude-code/index.d.ts`, the API; `claude-code-tools/index.d.ts`, so `e.tool === \"Bash\"` narrows `e`; `claude-code-mcp/index.d.ts`, the MCP tools connected when the mod last reloaded; each `dependencies` plugin's contract; and a `tsconfig.json` the mod's own extends, so `tsc -p <mod folder>` type-checks it. The API file carries every event's input and result, every noun and method on `$` with its doc comment and an example, and every element's props, in about 14,000 lines: grep it for the name at hand (`'tool.call'`, `Pane: {`, `export type ToolCallResult`).\n\nWHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE TURN ENDS. Loading this skill through the Skill tool or its slash command starts the engine's watch on the folder that holds the mod folders. The first file written under it makes the engine ask the person, once, right then, while the turn goes on: \"Turn on the mods Claude writes in this session?\", with `How does this work?` first, then `Turn them on` and `Not now`. That question is the switch, and the person alone answers it: no permission mode, rule or hook does. On `Turn them on` the folder joins the session's plugin folders and the mod loads when the turn ends, whole, and each later edit reloads it when the turn that made the edit ends. The question stays up until answered. The answer reaches you as a notice at your next turn, which the engine may start for an enabling: enabled, with what the load came to; declined (the mods are written and load the next time this session starts; the person can ask for the question again); still open (dismissed or closed; asked again); or off, with the reason (nobody could be asked, as under `claude -p`; the question got no pick three times; an organization's policy; an untrusted workspace). A process that restarted loads an enabled folder again by itself; otherwise its watch starts the next time this skill loads, and a manifest already in the folder raises the question when that turn ends.\n\nA reload runs `register` and `session.start` again. `$.state` (the session's) and `$.store` (across sessions) stay; the module's own variables start over. New code meets old values: `shape`, in `reference.md`.\n\nTHIS SESSION is drawn in a terminal. A pane the person opened seats at any width; one opened unasked seats from 144 columns, or 110 as `$.ui.open`'s doc says, and waits below that. Where hot reloading is off as nobody could be asked, `claude --plugin-dir <mod folder>` starts a session with the mod loaded. This session has the `PublishPlugin` tool, so the normal way to share a mod here is the person's organization's library on claude.ai: when they ask to share or publish one, publish it with that tool, which shows them every file and sends nothing before their yes. A GitHub repository, by Sharing a mod in reference.md, is the other road: for people outside the organization, or when the person asks for a repository. Once the mod has loaded and the person has seen it do what they asked, offer once, in one line, to publish the mod to the organization's library; until then, sharing goes unmentioned unless the person raises it. Wait for their answer; a GitHub repository is for when the person asks for one. Say \"the mod\", not \"it\". Where the person says no, that answer stands for the rest of this session.\n\n## A mod in one paragraph\n\n`on(event, matcher?, hook)` adds a hook, and every hook is `($, e, next)`: `$` is the engine interface, each call spelled noun then method; `e` is the event's input, a plain frozen value; `next(e)` runs the plugins beneath and then the engine's own behaviour, resolving to the event's result. A hook that returns without `next` answers for itself; `next({ ...e, x })` rewrites what the rest sees. The module has no DOM or Node: `$` reaches everything outside it. JSX compiles against the global `h`, and the elements come from the drawing surface's own table, `const { Box, Text, Button } = $.ui.resolve(e)`, where `e.surface` is `terminal`, `desktop`, `vscode` or `mobile`.\n\n## From the ask to the shape\n\nWhen a person asks for a panel, or for anything kept beside the chat, build a pane as a mod, not a page, even if what it shows is fixed. A page is for a document to read or send.\n\nEach example, in `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/examples/`, is one complete hooks module; with the two JSON files above and any contract, it loads, validates and type-checks on this build.\n\n| The person asks for | What it is | Shown in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a pane, panel, sidebar, live view | `$.ui.open({ id, title })`, drawn by a `ui.render` hook on `{ component: 'Pane', requestId: id }`; it resolves `{ isPlaced }`, whether a surface draws it now | `pane.tsx`, its contract `pane-state.d.ts` |\n| a band or row above the prompt | a `ui.render` hook on `{ component: 'AbovePrompt' }` returning a tree, or `next(e)` with nothing to show | `band.tsx`, its contract `band-state.d.ts` |\n| a status line entry | `$.ui.status(text)` from any hook; `undefined` clears it | `tool-call.ts` |\n| a toast | `$.ui.toast(text)` from any hook | `band.tsx` |\n| block, rewrite or react to a tool call | `on('tool.call', { tool }, hook)`: return `{ deny }`, call `next({ ...e, ... })`, or `await next(e)` and act on the result | `tool-call.ts` |\n| change or react to a prompt | `on('prompt.submit', hook)`: `next({ ...e, text })` | `band.tsx` |\n| change the system prompt | `on('prompt.compose', hook)`: answer `next(e)`'s `{ sections }` with one added last (`scope: 'session'`), replaced or dropped | its doc in the types |\n| play a sound | `$.audio.play({ asset })` from any hook, the asset a file of the mod's | its doc in the types |\n| a slash command | `$.command.register({ name, description })` in `session.start`, answered by a `command.run` hook returning `{ text }` | `pane.tsx` |\n| values a drawing reads | `atom(ref, initial)`, `read($, atom)` while drawing, `update($, atom, fn)` from a handler or another event; the write redraws the readers; each value declared in the contract | `pane-state.d.ts` |\n| work on a timer, a tool the model calls, a subagent type, model calls, files, processes | `$.clock`, `$.tool`, `$.agent`, `$.model`, `$.fs`, `$.process` | `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/reference.md` |\n\n## Checking it and reading what the engine refused\n\n`claude plugin validate <mod folder>` reads the manifest and the module's source the way the engine will, and reports what the module hooks and calls and all the engine would refuse. Type-check the mod: with its types laid, `tsc -p <mod folder>`; else (the first write, `claude -p`, an SDK host), with the `tsconfig.json` from the types file's header, kept outside the mod folder, its `include` naming that file and the mod's `hooks`. Test what was asked in a `*.test.ts` like the example `pane.test.tsx`, seeding what an earlier version stored: run `claude plugin test <mod folder>`. A finished mod is at its full path, loaded or not by the hot-reloading answer: enabled, it is loaded when the turn ends, with nothing to run; any other answer, it is not loaded.\n\nWhile the session hot-reloads a plugin folder the transcript carries one dim line naming the plugin, the event and the reason when a hook fails or a module does not load, and a hook whose tree does not validate is such a hook, skipped alone: `<plugin>: ui.render hook skipped: returned the wrong shape (a tree <Component> refuses: <reason>)`. A failed hook of a mod you wrote comes back to you as a notice at your next turn. In any other session those lines go to the debug log alone, as `<plugin>: <line>`. The debug log (`claude --debug`) carries a line for every occurrence and every result the engine refused.\n\n`/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/82524c5ca115381f88f92466e534bde9/plugin-authoring/reference.md` is the long form: the full event list, streaming events, `ui.render` in depth, `$.state` contracts, `--plugin-dir` and `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` while developing, `userConfig` options, what a test holds, and Sharing a mod, the GitHub road, done when the person asks, or says yes where this skill offers it: `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` in its repository and, in the README, `/plugin install <mod> --marketplace <owner>/<repo>`.\n\n## User Request\n\nMake the existing todo-board mod (devenv/.claude/mods/todo-board) show the board LIVE: a persistent pane/band visible without typing /todo, auto-refreshing when .claude/todo-board.json changes.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6e41b4d8-b432-41b0-bc86-517a25b228fa","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus"}
