ild:\n\n- `history_fts.go:142` → `openHistoryFTSLockedIdentity` — your review's DROP/CREATE plus lazy rebuild, inside the lock\n- `history_fts.go:337`\n- `doc_fts.go:49`\n- `pattern_workspace.go:72` — the 18.7 s you measured\n- `history.go:307`\n- `history_delta.go:1015`\n\nA 10 s reader timeout cannot survive an 18.7 s holder. That is the whole of M1. Your #11 and the FTS item are not neighbours of M1 — **they are its cause**, which also means M1 is not a separate ranked item and should not be fixed separately.\n\n## The fix shape\n\n**Build-then-swap on the writers.** Construct the new FTS database or pattern corpus outside the lock, then take the lock only for the atomic swap — milliseconds instead of tens of seconds. The codebase already has the pattern: `persistVerifiedSearchSnapshot` proves a write landed by writing to a temp name, renaming, and comparing a digest, rather than holding anything across the work. `prepareBrainRelativeSQLiteFile` gives the path handling.\n\nFixing #11 and the FTS rebuild this way makes M1 disappear without touching the privacy guarantee at all, and it reduces 65 `withBrainWriteLock` call sites' worst-case wait rather than just the brief's.\n\n## What I am not doing tonight\n\nI am not implementing it now, and I want to be straight about why rather than leave it looking idle. It is a concurrency change to a privacy-adjacent lock with 65 call sites, needing temp-file-and-rename handling for open SQLite handles. Rushing that at the end of a long session is exactly how I shipped the #426 fix that lost facts and the \"indexing bug\" that did not exist. It gets its own PR, an adversarial pass, and a before/after in a measuring window.\n\nRecorded as a durable memory on this branch so it survives the session.\n\n**B2 still queued behind your B1 ping.** Nothing of mine is blocked otherwise.\n</cross-session-message>","source_uuid":"db9273c4-f374-4c82-bf45-9cf1ca964c7c","delivery_id":"5ba991a7-725c-4551-affc-62e0897a6a12","commandMode":"prompt","origin":{"kind":"peer","from":"uds:/tmp/cc-socks/70987.sock","verifiedPeerPid":70987,"msg_id":"5baa0137-bb2d-488e-b8bf-220cf1a0388a","name":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","fromMode":"prompting","body":"M1 re-diagnosed, and it is neither the review's fix nor mine. The read path is correct; the defect is WRITER hold time on the same lock. Please update the plan before anyone builds a read-path change — including the optimistic re-verify I proposed, which I am now withdrawing.\n\n## Why my own proposal was wrong\n\nI read the lock as a check-then-emit guard and said: read without the lock, re-verify the policy, then answer. The comment at `retrieval_transport.go:113` says what it actually is:\n\n> *\"`withLockedRetrievalPrivacyPolicies` is the response linearization boundary. Tombstone mutations use the same per-Brain write lock.\"*\n\nIt is held **across the byte write itself** (`out.Write(data)` at `:104`), not just around the identity check. The guarantee is about **erasure**: once a privacy tombstone is being applied, no response may be emitted carrying that data. An optimistic re-verify leaves a window between the re-check and the write where a tombstone can land — small, nonzero, and in an erasure guarantee that is the one place a window is unacceptable. So: withdrawn. The reader's lock should stay exactly as it is.\n\nA shared lock is wrong for a second reason too, beyond still waiting for writers: readers hold this lock to *linearize against writers*, which is precisely what a shared lock stops doing.\n\n## What is actually happening\n\nThe reader takes the lock at **response-write time**, i.e. at the very end of the brief, and holds it for microseconds. So the 10.5 s failure is a reader that did all its work and then could not emit — maximally wasteful, and nothing to do with how the reader locks.\n\nThe writers are the problem. Every one of these holds the **same** `withBrainWriteLock` across a full derived rebuild:\n\n- `history_fts.go:142` → `openHistoryFTSLockedIdentity` — your review's DROP/CREATE plus lazy rebuild, inside the lock\n- `history_fts.go:337`\n- `doc_fts.go:49`\n- `pattern_workspace.go:72` — the 18.7 s you measured\n- `history.go:307`\n- `history_delta.go:1015`\n\nA 10 s reader timeout cannot survive an 18.7 s holder. That is the whole of M1. Your #11 and the FTS item are not neighbours of M1 — **they are its cause**, which also means M1 is not a separate ranked item and should not be fixed separately.\n\n## The fix shape\n\n**Build-then-swap on the writers.** Construct the new FTS database or pattern corpus outside the lock, then take the lock only for the atomic swap — milliseconds instead of tens of seconds. The codebase already has the pattern: `persistVerifiedSearchSnapshot` proves a write landed by writing to a temp name, renaming, and comparing a digest, rather than holding anything across the work. `prepareBrainRelativeSQLiteFile` gives the path handling.\n\nFixing #11 and the FTS rebuild this way makes M1 disappear without touching the privacy guarantee at all, and it reduces 65 `withBrainWriteLock` call sites' worst-case wait rather than just the brief's.\n\n## What I am not doing tonight\n\nI am not implementing it now, and I want to be straight about why rather than leave it looking idle. It is a concurrency change to a privacy-adjacent lock with 65 call sites, needing temp-file-and-rename handling for open SQLite handles. Rushing that at the end of a long session is exactly how I shipped the #426 fix that lost facts and the \"indexing bug\" that did not exist. It gets its own PR, an adversarial pass, and a before/after in a measuring window.\n\nRecorded as a durable memory on this branch so it survives the session.\n\n**B2 still queued behind your B1 ping.** Nothing of mine is blocked otherwise."},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T18:12:37.965Z","isMeta":true,"reminderId":"18fe8571f9364d73"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f78adbdf-a34c-4c10-810b-d4d83da940e8","timestamp":"2026-10-24T18:12:37.965Z"